  "response": "Actinomycetin (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC518278/)\nciteturn29search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 79.9 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; 1947 Jan;53(1):101–102.\n\n# Actinomycetin\n\nMaurice Welsch\n\n[Button: J Bacteriol]\n\n. 1947 Jan;53(1):101–102.\n\n# Actinomycetin\n\nMaurice Welsch\n\n### Maurice Welsch\n\n^{1}Laboratory of Bacteriology and Parasitology, University of Liège, Belgium\n\nFind articles by Maurice Welsch\n\n^{1}\n\n  * [Button: Author information]\n  * [Button: Copyright and License information]\n\n^{1}Laboratory of Bacteriology and Parasitology, University of Liège, Belgium\n\nPMC Copyright notice\n\nPMCID: PMC518278  PMID: 16561239\n\n## Full text\n\nPDF\n\n  * Previous\n  * 101\n\n## Selected References\n\nThese references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article.\n\n  1. Welsch M. Bacteriolytic Properties of Actinomyces. J Bacteriol. 1941 Dec;42(6):801–814. doi: 10.1128/jb.42.6.801-814.1941. [DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n  2. Welsch M. Bacteriostatic and Bacteriolytic Properties of Actinomycetes. J Bacteriol. 1942 Nov;44(5):571–588. doi: 10.1128/jb.44.5.571-588.1942. [DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nActinomycetin (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC518278/?page=0)\nciteturn29search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 79.9 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; 1947 Jan;53(1):101–102.\n\n# Actinomycetin\n\nMaurice Welsch\n\n[Button: J Bacteriol]\n\n. 1947 Jan;53(1):101–102.\n\n# Actinomycetin\n\nMaurice Welsch\n\n### Maurice Welsch\n\n^{1}Laboratory of Bacteriology and Parasitology, University of Liège, Belgium\n\nFind articles by Maurice Welsch\n\n^{1}\n\n  * [Button: Author information]\n  * [Button: Copyright and License information]\n\n^{1}Laboratory of Bacteriology and Parasitology, University of Liège, Belgium\n\nPMC Copyright notice\n\nPMCID: PMC518278  PMID: 16561239\n\n## Full text\n\nPDF\n\n  * Previous\n\n## Selected References\n\nThese references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article.\n\n  1. Welsch M. Bacteriolytic Properties of Actinomyces. J Bacteriol. 1941 Dec;42(6):801–814. doi: 10.1128/jb.42.6.801-814.1941. [DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n  2. Welsch M. Bacteriostatic and Bacteriolytic Properties of Actinomycetes. J Bacteriol. 1942 Nov;44(5):571–588. doi: 10.1128/jb.44.5.571-588.1942. [DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComparative genome-scale metabolic reconstruction and flux balance analysis of multiple Staphylococcus aureus genomes identify novel antimicrobial drug targets (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19376871/)\nciteturn29search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Comparative genome-scale metabolic reconstruction and flux balance analysis of multiple Staphylococcus aureus genomes identify novel antimicrobial drug targets ...   * PMID: 19376871 ...   * PMID: 19376871 ... Mortality due to multidrug-resistant Staphylococcus aureus infection is predicted to surpass that of human immunodeficiency virus/AIDS in the United States. ... We thus conclude that metabolic reconstruction and in silico analyses of multiple strains of the same bacterial species provide a novel approach for potential antibiotic target identification. ... AAM^{−} is a modified minimal medium derived from metabolic reconstruction; AAM^{+} is a published minimal medium (27).\n\n. 2009 Jun;191(12):4015-24.\n\ndoi: 10.1128/JB.01743-08. Epub 2009 Apr 17.\n\n# Comparative genome-scale metabolic reconstruction and flux balance analysis of multiple Staphylococcus aureus genomes identify novel antimicrobial drug targets\n\nDeok-Sun Lee^{  1 }, Henry Burd, Jiangxia Liu, Eivind Almaas, Olaf Wiest, Albert-László Barabási, Zoltán N Oltvai, Vinayak Kapatral\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliation\n\n  * ^{1} Center for Complex Network Research, Northeastern University, Boston, Massachusetts 022151, USA.\n\n  * PMID: 19376871\n  * PMCID: PMC2698402\n  * DOI: 10.1128/JB.01743-08\n\n# Comparative genome-scale metabolic reconstruction and flux balance analysis of multiple Staphylococcus aureus genomes identify novel antimicrobial drug targets\n\nDeok-Sun Lee et al. J Bacteriol. 2009 Jun.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat [Select]\n\n[Button: J Bacteriol ]\n\nActions\n\n  * Search in PubMed\n  * Search in NLM Catalog\n  * Add to Search\n\n. 2009 Jun;191(12):4015-24.\n\ndoi: 10.1128/JB.01743-08. Epub 2009 Apr 17.\n\n### Authors\n\nDeok-Sun Lee^{  1 }, Henry Burd, Jiangxia Liu, Eivind Almaas, Olaf Wiest, Albert-László Barabási, Zoltán N Oltvai, Vinayak Kapatral\n\n### Affiliation\n\n  * ^{1} Center for Complex Network Research, Northeastern University, Boston, Massachusetts 022151, USA.\n\n  * PMID: 19376871\n  * PMCID: PMC2698402\n  * DOI: 10.1128/JB.01743-08\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n[Button: Full text links ]Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat [Select]\n\n## Abstract\n\nMortality due to multidrug-resistant Staphylococcus aureus infection is predicted to surpass that of human immunodeficiency virus/AIDS in the United States. Despite the various treatment options for S. aureus infections, it remains a major hospital- and community-acquired opportunistic pathogen. With the emergence of multidrug-resistant S. aureus strains, there is an urgent need for the discovery of new antimicrobial drug targets in the organism. To this end, we reconstructed the metabolic networks of multidrug-resistant S. aureus strains using genome annotation, functional-pathway analysis, and comparative genomic approaches, followed by flux balance analysis-based in silico single and double gene deletion experiments. We identified 70 single enzymes and 54 pairs of enzymes whose corresponding metabolic reactions are predicted to be unconditionally essential for growth. Of these, 44 single enzymes and 10 enzyme pairs proved to be common to all 13 S. aureus strains, including many that had not been previously identified as being essential for growth by gene deletion experiments in S. aureus. We thus conclude that metabolic reconstruction and in silico analyses of multiple strains of the same bacterial species provide a novel approach for potential antibiotic target identification.\n\nPubMed Disclaimer\n\n## Figures\n\nFIG. 1.\n\nSingle essential and synthetic-lethal enzymes…\n\nFIG. 1.\n\nSingle essential and synthetic-lethal enzymes of the S. aureus metabolic network and their…\n\nFIG. 1.\n\nSingle essential and synthetic-lethal enzymes of the S. aureus metabolic network and their strain dependence. (a) Subnetworks of 44 strain-independent single essential enzymes of multiple S. aureus strains. Two nodes (enzymes) are connected if their reactions involve common metabolites. The EC number and the associated genes are presented for each node. The node colors correspond to the metabolic pathways the enzymes belong to. (b) Subset of the amino sugar pathway involving two single essential enzymes, EC 2.5.1.7 and EC1.1.1.158. Three pairs of enzymes, EC 2.3.1.157-EC 5.1.3.14, EC 2.7.7.23-EC 5.1.3.14, and EC 5.4.2.10-EC 5.1.3.14, are synthetic-lethal pairs in all strains. The enzymes in each pair individually do not affect growth (biomass production), but their simultaneous inactivation/deletion causes lethality. (c) Distribution of the numbers of strains in which an enzyme is singly essential. Altogether, 70 enzymes were identified as singly essential in at least one strain. Of these, more than 60% are found in all the S. aureus strains. (d) Distribution of the numbers of strains in which the deletion of a pair of enzymes is synthetic lethal. A total of 54 synthetic-lethal pairs were identified in at least one strain. About 40% of them are found only in one strain, reflecting the strain specificity of synthetic-lethal pairs, with less than 20% being present in all the strains.\n\nFIG. 2.\n\nExperimental validation of S.aureus growth…\n\nFIG. 2.\n\nExperimental validation of S.aureus growth in metabolic-reconstruction-based minimal medium. Shown are the growth…\n\nFIG. 2.\n\nExperimental validation of S.aureus growth in metabolic-reconstruction-based minimal medium. Shown are the growth curves of S. aureus Mu50 (a), S. aureus USA300 (b), and an S. aureus patient isolate (c). AAM^{−} is a modified minimal medium derived from metabolic reconstruction; AAM^{+} is a published minimal medium (27).\n\n[Button: ] [Button: ]\n\nSee this image and copyright information in PMC\n\n## References\n\n  1.     1. Almaas, E., B. Kovacs, T. Vicsek, Z. N. Oltvai, and A.-L. Barabási. 2004. Global organization of metabolic fluxes in the bacterium Escherichia coli. Nature 427839-843. - PubMed\n  2.     1. Almaas, E., Z. N. Oltvai, and A.-L. Barabási. 2005. The activity reaction core and plasticity of metabolic networks. PLoS Comp. Biol. 10557-0563. - PMC - PubMed\n  3.     1. Baba, T., T. Bae, O. Schneewind, F. Takeuchi, and K. Hiramatsu. 2008. Genome sequence of Staphylococcus aureus strain Newman and comparative analysis of staphylococcal genomes: polymorphism and evolution of two major pathogenicity islands. J. Bacteriol. 190300-310. - PMC - PubMed\n  4.     1. Becker, S. A., and B. O. Palsson. 2005. Genome-scale reconstruction of the metabolic network in Staphylococcus aureus N315: an initial draft to the two dimensional annotation. BMC Microbiol. 58-20. - PMC - PubMed\n  5.     1. Bhattacharyya, A., S. Stilwagen, G. Reznik, H. Feil, W. Feil, I. Anderson, A. Bernal, M. D' Souza, N. Ivanova, V. Kapatral, N. Larsen, T. Los, A. Lykidis, E. J. Selkov, T. L. Walunas, A. Purcell, R. A. Edwards, T. Hawkins, R. Haselkorn, R. Overbeek, N. C. Kyrpides, and P. Predki. 2002. Draft sequencing and comparative genomics of Xylella fastidiosa subspecies reveal novel biological insights. Genome Res. 121556-1563. - PMC - PubMed\n\n[Button: Show all 30 references ]\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * [Button: Anti-Bacterial Agents / pharmacology* ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Bacterial Proteins / genetics ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Bacterial Proteins / metabolism ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Drug Discovery / methods* ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Genome, Bacterial ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Genomics / methods* ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Metabolic Networks and Pathways ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Metabolomics / methods* ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Staphylococcus aureus / drug effects ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Staphylococcus aureus / enzymology ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Staphylococcus aureus / genetics* ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search\n\n  * [Button: Staphylococcus aureus / metabolism* ]\n\nActions\n\n    * Search in PubMed\n    * Search in MeSH\n    * Add to Search--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nActinomycetin (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC518278/?page=1)\nciteturn29search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; 1947 Jan;53(1):101–102.\n\n# Actinomycetin\n\nMaurice Welsch\n\n. 1947 Jan;53(1):101–102.\n\n# Actinomycetin\n\nMaurice Welsch\n\n### Maurice Welsch\n\n^{1}Laboratory of Bacteriology and Parasitology, University of Liège, Belgium\n\nFind articles by Maurice Welsch\n\n^{1}\n\n  * [Button: Author information]\n  * [Button: Copyright and License information]\n\n^{1}Laboratory of Bacteriology and Parasitology, University of Liège, Belgium\n\nPMC Copyright notice\n\nPMCID: PMC518278  PMID: 16561239\n\n## Full text\n\n## Selected References\n\nThese references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article.\n\n  1. Welsch M. Bacteriolytic Properties of Actinomyces. J Bacteriol. 1941 Dec;42(6):801–814. doi: 10.1128/jb.42.6.801-814.1941. [DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n  2. Welsch M. Bacteriostatic and Bacteriolytic Properties of Actinomycetes. J Bacteriol. 1942 Nov;44(5):571–588. doi: 10.1128/jb.44.5.571-588.1942. [DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nActinomycetin (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC518278/?page=3)\nciteturn29search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 79.9 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; 1947 Jan;53(1):101–102.\n\n# Actinomycetin\n\nMaurice Welsch\n\n[Button: J Bacteriol]\n\n. 1947 Jan;53(1):101–102.\n\n# Actinomycetin\n\nMaurice Welsch\n\n### Maurice Welsch\n\n^{1}Laboratory of Bacteriology and Parasitology, University of Liège, Belgium\n\nFind articles by Maurice Welsch\n\n^{1}\n\n  * [Button: Author information]\n  * [Button: Copyright and License information]\n\n^{1}Laboratory of Bacteriology and Parasitology, University of Liège, Belgium\n\nPMC Copyright notice\n\nPMCID: PMC518278  PMID: 16561239\n\n## Full text\n\nPDF\n\n  * Previous\n  * 101\n  * 102\n  * Next\n\n## Selected References\n\nThese references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article.\n\n  1. Welsch M. Bacteriolytic Properties of Actinomyces. J Bacteriol. 1941 Dec;42(6):801–814. doi: 10.1128/jb.42.6.801-814.1941. [DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n  2. Welsch M. Bacteriostatic and Bacteriolytic Properties of Actinomycetes. J Bacteriol. 1942 Nov;44(5):571–588. doi: 10.1128/jb.44.5.571-588.1942. [DOI] [PMC free article] [PubMed] [Google Scholar]\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBacteriolytic Enzymes from Streptomycetes (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2195212/)\nciteturn29search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 64.7 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago;     tarian hypothesis (Welsch, 1947 a) according to which actinomycetin con-\n\n# Bacteriolytic Enzymes from Streptomycetes\n\nA review\n\nContributor: [contrib-type: author] [name-style: western] Welsch [initials: M] Maurice\n\nAffiliation: From The Laboratory Of General and Medical Microbiology, University of Liège, Liège, Belgium\n\nPublication date: [pub-type: ppub] Day: 1 Month: 3 Year: 1962\n\nVolume: 45 Issue: 4 Issue: [pub-id-type: pmc-issue-id] 158930 First page: 115 Last page: 124\n\n## Abstract\n\nA study of the bacteriolytic properties of streptomycetes has progressively uncovered the production by these microorganisms of a large number of different enzymes acting upon various bacterial constituents, especially on some of them located in the cell wall. Although the mechanism of the bacteriolysis is far from being completely elucidated at present, it can, however, be stated that, in two instances at least, it can be regarded as an osmotic explosion following upon the destruction of the structure responsible for the rigidity of the cell wall.\n    Leaving aside the often quoted but rather incidental observation of Gas-\n    perini (1890), it may be stated that the ability of Streptomyces spp. to lyse\n    the bacterial cells of diflerent species, either previously killed or alive, was\n    independently discovered by Lieske (1921) and by Gratia and Dath (1924).\n    The latter demonstrated that the lytic activity could be recovered in the cell-\n    free filtrate from a suitable actinomycete culture. Similar reports were pub-\n    lished during the following years (Rosenthal, 1925; Dath, 1927; Borodulina,\n    1935; Gratia and Alexander, 1931) and, later on, by analogy with the word\n    \"penicillin,\" as originally introduced by Fleming (1929), we proposed to\n    designate such bacteriolyfic cell-free fluids as actinomycetin (Welsch, 1937 c).\n    The actinomycetin type of lytic activity, which is quite widespread among\n    Actinomycetes (Welsch, 1942, 1947 c; Bergamini, Ghuysen, and Welsch,\n    1954), can be described as follows :--\n    (a) The active actinomycete, when planted in a suspension of properly\n    killed bacteria belonging to any one of a large number of species tested, will\n    grow and dissolve the bacterial bodies.\n    (b) The same result obtains when the actinomycete is planted in a suspen-\n    sion of living bacteria belonging to a wide variety of Gram-positive species.\n    (c) The cell-free fluid, obtained after centrifugation or filtration of a culture\n    of the actinomycete, in short, actinomycetin, shows the bacteriolytic ac-\n    tivities described under (a) and (b), although its effects upon living bacteria\n    are often somewhat more restricted and may be rather irregular.\n    A microorganism, which we isolated in 1936 (Welsch, 1936) and which\n    Ix5\n    The Journal of General Physiology\n    II6 THE JOURNAL OF GENERAL PHYSIOLOGY * VOLUME 45 \" 1962\n    was described later on as Streptomyces albus G (Waksman, Horning, Welsch,\n    and Woodruff, 1942; Welsch, 1947 c), maintained throughout the years in\n    a suitably active state by selection procedures, was used in our laboratory\n    as the typical actinomycetin producer. In many instances, however, results\n    obtained with the aforementioned organism were extended to a large number\n    of other strains, mostly unidentified, maintained as a reference culture col-\n    lection.\n    1. First Hypothesis: A Single Lytic Enzyme\n    Early studies, bearing on the physical and chemical properties of the agent\n    of actinomycetin G responsible for its lytic action on killed bacterial bodies,\n    strongly suggested that it was a protein, while the kinetics of bacteriolysis\n    indicated that it very likely acted as an enzyme (Welsch, 1937 b, 1947 c,\n    Welsch and Elford, 1937). Its purification was therefore attempted, using\n    the methods devised by Northrop (1939) and his collaborators for the isola-\n    tion of crystalline enzymes. Unfortunately, the exceedingly low concentration\n    of active protein in actinomycetin proved to be a very serious handicap to\n    its purification by the salting-out technique. However, the activity of the\n    still very crude but concentrated preparations thus obtained, when compared\n    with that of crystalline trypsin and other highly purified proteases against\n    various substrates, showed a specificity for killed bacterial bodies which\n    having been found in the absence of the colilytic type, we proposed a uni-\n    tarian hypothesis (Welsch, 1947 a) according to which actinomycetin con-\n    tained only a single bacteriolytic agent, namely actinozyme. Then, in order\n    to explain its action upon living Gram-positive bacteria (Welsch, 1937 a;\n    Swertz, 1949; Smoliar, 1950), which is clearly a manifestation of antibiosis\n    (Welsch, 1947 b), we were led to assume that a bactericidal agent was present\n    MAtIRICEWELSCtt Bacteriolytic\n    EnzymesfromStreptomycetes II7\n    in actinomycetin, which first killed the microorganisms thus making them\n    sensitive to the lytic effect of actinozyme. The kinetics of staphylolysis (Welsch,\n    1947 c) could easily be interpreted along those lines and, furthermore, a\n    material of a lipoid nature, specifically bactericidal for Gram-positive bac-\n    teria, was extracted from actinomycetin, although it was also found, albeit\n    in much smaller amounts, in the sterile culture medium (Welsch, 1941).\n    explanation, in the case of actinomycetin G, on account of its very weak\n    action on M. lysodeikticus, then generally considered as the most sensitive\n    substrate (Welsch, 1942, 1947 c).\n    McCarty was therefore entitled to conclude that streptolysis was due to,\n    or at least initiated by, a specific non-proteolytic enzyme, other than ribo-\n    MAURmEWELSCH BacteriolyticEnzymesfromStreptomycetes II9\n    nuclease or lysozyme, complete clearing of the streptococcal suspensions\n    requiring, however, the participation of a non-specific protease. He next dem-\n    onstrated that the streptolytic enzyme dissolved the isolated and purified pro-\n    tease-resistant cell walls of streptococci, acting very likely on their carbohy-\n    drate moiety. Thus was introduced the notion that bacteriolysis by strepto-\n    mycetes might well be the consequence of a specific enzymic destruction of the\n    bacterial cell wall.\n    McCarty's work prompted us to investigate whether his streptolytic and\n    our staphylolytic enzyme were identical. This led to the discovery that lysis\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA new antibiotic, grisamine. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/13242476/)\nciteturn29search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; # A new antibiotic, grisamine. ...   * T SAWAZAKI\n# A new antibiotic, grisamine.\n\nThe Journal of antibiotics\n\nPMID: 13242476\n\nPublication date: 1955 Apr\n\nPublication status: ppublish\n\nJournal abbreviation: J Antibiot (Tokyo)\n\nJournal country: England\n\nJournal NLM unique ID: 0151115\n\nJournal linking ISSN: 0021-8820\n\nISSN: 0021-8820\n\nVolume: 8\n\nIssue: 2\n\nPages: 39-41\n\n## Authors\n\n  * T SAWAZAKI\n  * G NAKAMURA\n  * M KAWASAKI\n  * S YAMASHITA\n  * K ISONO\n  * K ANZAI\n  * Y SERIZAWA\n  * Y SEKIYAMA\n  * S SUZUKI\n\n## Languages\n\n  * eng\n\n## Publication types\n\n  * Journal Article\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Anti-Bacterial Agents\n  * Streptomyces\n\n## Keywords\n\n  * ANTIBIOTICS\n  * STREPTOMYCES\n\n## Substances\n\n  * Anti-Bacterial Agents--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComparative Genome-Scale Metabolic Reconstruction and Flux Balance Analysis of Multiple Staphylococcus aureus Genomes Identify Novel Antimicrobial Drug Targets (https://journals.asm.org/doi/full/10.1128/jb.01743-08)\nciteturn29search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; PubMed: 19376871 ... Methicillin-Resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) ... Microbiome Research ... AAM^{−} is a modified minimal medium derived from metabolic reconstruction; AAM^{+} is a published minimal medium (27).\n### Information\n\n#### Published In\n\nImage: cover image Journal of Bacteriology\n\nJournal of Bacteriology\n\nVolume 191 • Number 12 • 15 June 2009\n\nPages: 4015 - 4024\n\nPubMed: 19376871\n\n#### Copyright\n\n© 2009 American Society for Microbiology.\n\n#### History\n\nReceived: 12 December 2008\n\nAccepted: 25 March 2009\n\nPublished online: 15 June 2009\n\n#### Topics\n\n  1. Antimicrobial Resistance\n  2. Antimicrobial Resistance\n  3. Antimicrobial Resistance\n  4. Bacterial Genomics\n  5. Bacterial Infections\n  6. Bacterial Pathogenesis\n  7. Bacteriology\n  8. Clinical Microbiology and Infectious Diseases\n  9. Drug Resistant Pathogens\n  10. Emerging Resistant Pathogens\n  11. Methicillin-Resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA)\n  12. Microbial Genomics\n  13. Microbial Pathogenesis and Immunology\n  14. Microbiome Research\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1958  TUBERCULOSIS CHEMOTHERAPY IN JAPAN  91 (https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/yoken1952/11/3/11_3_87/_pdf)\nciteturn29search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.9 years ago; Crawled: 16.7 years ago; Grisamine: Grisamine is an antibiotic produced by a strain of streptomyces similar to S. griseoflavus (Sawazaki et al., 1955). ... Grisamine inhibited the growth of BCG and virulent tubercle bacilli at the concentration of 5 μg per cc in Kirchner liquid medium.\nHowever, no further investigation was reported thereafter.\n\nGrisamine: Grisamine is an antibiotic produced by a strain of streptomyces similar to S. griseoflavus (Sawazaki et al., 1955). It was obtained in the form of needle crystals melted at 167°C to 170°C having C20H30O7 as its empirical formula. Grisamine inhibited the growth of BCG and virulent tubercle bacilli at the concentration of 5 μg per cc in Kirchner liquid medium. Intravenous injection of 160 mg per kg caused the death of mice within one week, but 150 mg per kg was tolerated. Miyamoto and others (1955) conducted mice protection test against experimental tuberculosis with this antibiotic. Thirty mice of an inbred strain were infected with 0.001 mg of virulent tubercle bacilli (H2 strain). Grisamine administration to 10 of them was initiated one day after infection with 30 mg daily dose, and continued for 3 weeks. The effect was evaluated by viable bacterial unit count of the lung, liver and spleen, comparing with other two groups of 10 each. One group had been treated with streptomycin and another group had been left untreated. The result indicated that grisamine has no effect on mouse inoculation tuberculosis.\n\nHomomycin: Homomycin was first isolated by Sumiki and his co-workers (1955) from the culture liquid of a new streptomyces named S. noboritensis. This antibiotic was reported to have a high activity specifically against H37Rv tubercle bacilli and BCG. In addition, it was proved that homomycin has almost no acute toxicity, LD50 being more than 2,000 mg per kg of mouse body weight by intravenous injection. These informations led Yanagisawa and others (1956) to an attempt to confirm the effect of homomycin in vitro and to extend the observation on mice experimental tuberculosis. The result was that homomycin inhibited the growth of H37Rv strain and its variant substrains resistant to streptomycin and isoniazid in Kirchner liquid medium at 1 μg per cc or less than that, but it failed to prolong significantly survival time of heavily infected tuberculous mice. Nevertheless, these authors proceeded to a larger scale of guinea--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComparative Genome-Scale Metabolic Reconstruction and Flux Balance Analysis of Multiple Staphylococcus aureus Genomes Identify Novel Antimicrobial Drug Targets (https://journals.asm.org/doi/10.1128/jb.01743-08)\nciteturn29search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; PubMed: 19376871 ... Center for Complex Network Research and Departments of Physics, Biology, and Computer Science, Northeastern University, Boston, Massachusetts 02215\n## Information & Contributors\n\n[Button: Information][Button: Contributors]\n\n### Information\n\n#### Published In\n\nImage: cover image Journal of Bacteriology\n\nJournal of Bacteriology\n\nVolume 191 • Number 12 • 15 June 2009\n\nPages: 4015 - 4024\n\nPubMed: 19376871\n\n#### Copyright\n\n© 2009 American Society for Microbiology.\n\n#### History\n\nReceived: 12 December 2008\n\nAccepted: 25 March 2009\n\nPublished online: 15 June 2009\n\nDownload PDF\n\n### Contributors\n\nExpand All\n\n#### Authors\n\n##### Deok-Sun Lee\n\nCenter for Complex Network Research and Departments of Physics, Biology, and Computer Science, Northeastern University, Boston, Massachusetts 02215\n\nCenter for Cancer Systems Biology, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Boston, Massachusetts 02215\n\nDepartment of Natural Medical Sciences, Inha University, Incheon, 402-751, Republic of Korea\n\nView all articles by Deok-Sun Lee\n\n##### Henry Burd\n\nIntegrated Genomics, Inc., Chicago, Illinois 60612\n\nView all articles by Henry Burd\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nComparative Genome-Scale Metabolic Reconstruction and Flux Balance Analysis of Multiple Staphylococcus aure